var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Functions","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Type flexibility","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Type-flexibility","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"All functions in this package support generic numeric types. Instead of being restricted to Float64, the functions accept any AbstractFloat (or Real) input type and preserve the type through computation where possible. This means you can use Float32 for faster computation with lower precision, or BigFloat for arbitrary-precision calculations."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"Examples:"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"# Float32 inputs → Float32 output\ndebye_function(2.0f0, 1.0f0, 5.0f0)  # returns Float32\n\n# BigFloat for high precision\ndebye_function(big\"2.0\", big\"1.0\", big\"5.0\")  # returns BigFloat\n\n# Integer inputs are automatically promoted to Float64\nU(0, 1)  # returns Float64\n\n# Mixed types promote to the widest common type\ndebye_function(2.0f0, 1.0, 5.0f0)  # Float32 + Float64 → Float64"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The following table summarizes the type behavior for each function family:"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"Function family Input type constraint Type preservation\nCoulomb wave functions Number Via Julia's promotion rules\nDebye functions Real / AbstractFloat Full (Float32, Float64, BigFloat)\nFresnel integrals Number Full (Float32, Float64, BigFloat); real and complex inputs\nDawson integral Real Full (Float32, Float64, BigFloat)\nClausen functions Real for θ Promoted to AbstractFloat\nFermi-Dirac integrals Real Full (Float32, Float64, BigFloat)\nBose–Einstein integrals Real; integer or half-integer order Full (Float32, Float64, BigFloat)\nMarcum Q-function Real / Number Full (Float32, Float64)\nVoigt function Real Full (Float32, Float64, BigFloat)\nParabolic cylinder Real / AbstractFloat Full (Float32, Float64, BigFloat)"},{"title":"Automatic differentiation","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Automatic-differentiation","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"All functions support automatic differentiation via ForwardDiff.jl. The extension is a weak dependency and is loaded automatically when ForwardDiff is available."},{"title":"Differentiating Fresnel integrals","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Differentiating-Fresnel-integrals","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The derivative of FresnelC is analytically cos(πx²/2), which ForwardDiff recovers exactly:"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using FewSpecialFunctions, ForwardDiff\n\nx0 = 0.7\ndC = ForwardDiff.derivative(FresnelC, x0)\nexact = cos((π / 2) * x0^2)\nisapprox(dC, exact)  # true"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"Similarly for FresnelS:"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"dS = ForwardDiff.derivative(FresnelS, x0)\nisapprox(dS, sin((π / 2) * x0^2))  # true"},{"title":"Marcum Q-function","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Marcum-Q-function","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The derivative of MarcumQ(M, a, b) with respect to b is available analytically via dQdb. ForwardDiff gives the same result:"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"M, a, b = 2.0, 1.5, 3.0\ndQ_ad = ForwardDiff.derivative(b -> MarcumQ(M, a, b), b)\ndQ_exact = dQdb(M, a, b)\nisapprox(dQ_ad, dQ_exact)  # true"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"ForwardDiff can also differentiate with respect to a or M, where no closed-form is available:"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"# Gradient with respect to both a and b simultaneously\nf(v) = MarcumQ(2.0, v[1], v[2])\ng = ForwardDiff.gradient(f, [1.5, 3.0])\n# g[1] ≈ d/da MarcumQ,  g[2] ≈ dQdb(2.0, 1.5, 3.0)"},{"title":"Parabolic cylinder function","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Parabolic-cylinder-function","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The first derivative dU(a, x) is built in; ForwardDiff agrees with it. The second derivative follows from the parabolic cylinder ODE, U''(a,x) = (x²/4 + a) U(a,x), and ForwardDiff recovers it by differentiating dU:"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"a, x0 = 0.5, 1.2\n\n# First derivative\ndU_ad = ForwardDiff.derivative(x -> U(a, x), x0)\nisapprox(dU_ad, dU(a, x0))  # true\n\n# Second derivative via ForwardDiff of dU\nd2U_ad = ForwardDiff.derivative(x -> dU(a, x), x0)\nd2U_ode = (x0^2 / 4 + a) * U(a, x0)   # from the ODE\nisapprox(d2U_ad, d2U_ode)  # true"},{"title":"Coulomb wave functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Coulomb-wave-functions","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The Coulomb wave functions are solutions to the radial Schrödinger equation for a charged particle in a Coulomb potential. This package implements both the regular (F_ℓ(η, ρ)) and irregular (G_ℓ(η, ρ)) Coulomb wave functions, as well as auxiliary functions and normalization constants. The implementation follows the approach described in arXiv:1804.10976, using confluent hypergeometric functions and robust normalization. The functions are implemented for real and complex arguments, and special care is taken to ensure numerical stability across a wide range of parameters."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"F(ℓ, η, ρ): Computes the regular Coulomb wave function using the normalization constant and the confluent hypergeometric function. For real arguments, the function returns the real part.\nG(ℓ, η, ρ): Computes the irregular Coulomb wave function as a combination of outgoing and incoming solutions.\nC(ℓ, η): Returns the normalization constant for the regular solution.\nη(a, k): Computes the Coulomb parameter.\nH⁺ and H⁻: Outgoing and incoming Coulomb wave functions, respectively."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The implementation is robust for both small and large arguments, and auxiliary functions such as derivatives and normalization factors are also provided. See the reference for mathematical details."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings # hide\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" # hide\n\nplot_font = \"Computer Modern\" # hide\ndefault(fontfamily=plot_font,linewidth=2.5, framestyle=:box, label=nothing, grid=true,palette=:tab10) # hide\nx = range(0, stop=25, length=1000)\nplot(x, real(F.(0.0, 0.3, x)), label=L\"F_0(0.3,ρ)\")\nplot!(x, real(F.(0.0, -0.3, x)), label=L\"F_0(-0.3,ρ)\")\nxlabel!(L\"ρ\")\ntitle!(\"Regular Coulomb Wave Functions\")"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"Use a similar approach to plot the regular Coulomb functions for different a ell"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings # hide\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" # hide\n\nplot_font = \"Computer Modern\" # hide\ndefault(fontfamily=plot_font,linewidth=2.5, framestyle=:box, label=nothing, grid=true,palette=:tab10) # hide\nx = range(0, stop=25, length=1000)\nplot(x, real(F.(1e-5, 5.0, x)), label=L\"F_0(5.0,ρ)\", linewidth=2)\nplot!(x, real(F.(1.0, 5.0, x)), label=L\"F_1(5.0,ρ)\", linewidth=2)\nplot!(x, real(F.(2.0, 5.0, x)), label=L\"F_2(5.0,ρ)\", linewidth=2)\nplot!(x, real(F.(3.0, 5.0, x)), label=L\"F_3(5.0,ρ)\", linewidth=2)\ntitle!(\"Regular Coulomb Wave Functions for Different ℓ\")\nxlabel!(L\"ρ\")"},{"title":"Complex plots","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Complex-plots","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using DomainColoring, FewSpecialFunctions, Plots\n\ndomaincolor(z -> F(0,z,z), [-2, 2, 0, 5], grid=true)"},{"title":"Marcum Q-function","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Marcum-Q-function-2","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The Marcum Q-function is a generalized integral involving the modified Bessel function of the first kind. It is widely used in communications and radar signal processing. The implementation in this package is based on the methods described in arXiv:1311.0681v1, providing accurate results for a wide range of parameters."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"MarcumQ(μ, a, b): Computes the Marcum Q-function for order μ, non-centrality parameter a, and threshold b. The implementation uses series expansions, asymptotic expansions, and recurrence relations for efficiency and accuracy.\ndQdb(M, a, b): Computes the derivative of the Marcum Q-function with respect to b."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The code automatically selects the most appropriate algorithm depending on the input parameters."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings  # hide\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" # hide\n\nplot_font = \"Computer Modern\" # hide\ndefault(fontfamily=plot_font,linewidth=2.5, framestyle=:box, label=nothing, grid=true,palette=:tab10) # hide\nbs = collect(range(0.0,10,length=100))\nM1 = MarcumQ(1, 0.2, bs)\nM2 = MarcumQ(1, 1.3, bs)\nM3 = MarcumQ(1, 2.5, bs)\nM4 = MarcumQ(1, 4.7, bs)\n\nplot(bs, M1, label=L\"a=0.2\")\nplot!(bs, M2, label=L\"a=1.3\")\nplot!(bs, M3, label=L\"a=2.5\")\nplot!(bs, M4, label=L\"a=4.7\")\nplot!(xlabel=\"b\", ylabel=L\"Q(1,a,b)\", title=\"Marcum Q-function\")"},{"title":"Derivative of the Marcum Q-function","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Derivative-of-the-Marcum-Q-function","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings  # hide\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" # hide\n\nplot_font = \"Computer Modern\" # hide\ndefault(fontfamily=plot_font,linewidth=2.5, framestyle=:box, label=nothing, grid=true,palette=:tab10) # hide\n\nbs = collect(range(0.0,10,length=100))\nM1 = dQdb(1, 0.2, bs)\nplot(bs, M1, label=L\"a=0.2\")"},{"title":"Voigt function","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Voigt-function","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The real Voigt function voigt(x, y) is the convolution of a Gaussian and a Lorentzian profile, with nonnegative width parameter y."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings # hide\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" # hide\n\ndefault(fontfamily=\"Computer Modern\", linewidth=2.5, framestyle=:box, grid=true)\nx = range(-6, 6, length=1000)\nplot(x, voigt.(x, 0.1), label=L\"y=0.1\", xlabel=L\"x\", ylabel=L\"K(x,y)\",\n     title=\"Voigt function\")\nplot!(x, voigt.(x, 1.0), label=L\"y=1.0\")"},{"title":"Parabolic cylinder functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Parabolic-cylinder-functions","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The parabolic cylinder functions U(a, x) and V(a, x) solve the parabolic cylinder differential equation. U and W use asymptotic expansions only when their decreasing terms reach the target precision. Otherwise, they use a convergent series with extra working precision to control cancellation. This fallback favors accuracy over speed. The derivatives dU, dV, and dW differentiate the same expansions as their corresponding functions."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"U(a, x): Computes the parabolic cylinder function of the first kind using a combination of series and asymptotic expansions.\nV(a, x): Computes the second, linearly independent solution using a convergent series with extra working precision.\nW(a, x): Solves the related equation W=(a-x^24)W using series or asymptotic expansions."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The formulas follow DLMF Chapter 12, including its expansions for W."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"On Julia versions with process-global BigFloat precision (including Julia 1.10), the extra-precision fallback is serialized between cylinder calls. Avoid running it concurrently with unrelated BigFloat arithmetic, which shares that precision setting."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings # hide\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" # hide\n\nplot_font = \"Computer Modern\" # hide\ndefault(fontfamily=plot_font,linewidth=2.5, framestyle=:box, label=nothing, grid=true,palette=:tab10) # hide\nxs = collect(-2.5:0.05:2.5)\nplot(xs, U(0.5, xs), label=L\"a=0.5\")\nplot!(xs, U(2.0, xs), label=L\"a=2.0\")\nplot!(xs, U(3.5, xs), label=L\"a=3.5\")\nplot!(xs, U(5.0, xs), label=L\"a=5.0\")\nplot!(xs, U(8.0, xs), label=L\"a=8.0\")\nplot!(xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=L\"U(a,x)\", title=\"Parabolic Cylinder Function U(a,x)\")"},{"title":"V(a, x)","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#V(a,-x)","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The function V(a, x) is a second, linearly independent solution to the same differential equation satisfied by U(a, x). It uses a convergent series with extra working precision."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings  # hide\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" # hide\n\nplot_font = \"Computer Modern\" # hide\ndefault(fontfamily=plot_font,linewidth=2.5, framestyle=:box, label=nothing, grid=true,palette=:tab10) # hide\nxs = collect(range(-2.5,2.5,length=100))\nV1 = V(0.5, xs)\nV2 = V(2.0, xs)\nV3 = V(3.5, xs)\nV4 = V(5.0, xs)\n\nplot(xs, V1, label=L\"a=0.5\")\nplot!(xs, V2, label=L\"a=2.0\")\nplot!(xs, V3, label=L\"a=3.5\")\nplot!(xs, V4, label=L\"a=5.0\")\nylims!(-3.0, 3.0)  \nplot!(xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=L\"V(a,x)\", title=\"Parabolic cylinder function V(a,x)\")"},{"title":"Debye functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Debye-functions","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The Debye functions are given by"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"    D_n(betax)= fracnx^n int_0^x fract^n(texte^t-1)^beta  textdt"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"For finite n > 0, the integral converges when 0 < β < n+1. At x = 0, the limiting value is 0 for β < 1, 1 for β = 1, and Inf for β > 1."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The implementation uses transformed adaptive quadrature. tol specifies a relative tolerance, floored at eight times machine epsilon; max_terms limits the number of quadrature subintervals. Failure to reach that tolerance raises an error."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings # hide\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" # hide\n\nplot_font = \"Computer Modern\" # hide\ndefault(fontfamily=plot_font,linewidth=2.5, framestyle=:box, label=nothing, grid=true,palette=:tab10) # hide\nx = range(0,stop=25,length=1000)\nplot(x,debye_function(1.0,1.0,x),label=L\"D_1(x)\")\nplot!(x,debye_function(2.0,1.0,x),label=L\"D_2(x)\")\nplot!(x,debye_function(3.0,1.0,x), label=L\"D_3(x)\")\ntitle!(\"Debye Functions\")\nxlabel!(L\"x\")\nsavefig(\"debye.svg\"); nothing # hide"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"(Image: )"},{"title":"Fermi-Dirac integrals","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Fermi-Dirac-integrals","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"In solid state physics the Fermi-Dirac integral is given by"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"    F_j(x) = int_0^infty fract^jexp(t-x)+1  dt"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"Approximations to this and the normalized case for j=-12, j=12, j=32 and j=52 are implemented to varying accuacy. Most are of the order of 10^-12."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"Here is an example"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings # hide\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" # hide\n\nplot_font = \"Computer Modern\" # hide\ndefault(fontfamily=plot_font,linewidth=2.5, framestyle=:box, label=nothing, grid=true,palette=:tab10) # hide\nx = range(0,stop=100,length=10000)\nplot(x,FermiDiracIntegralNorm.(-1/2,x),label=L\"F_{-1/2}(x)\")\nplot!(x,FermiDiracIntegralNorm.(1/2,x),label=L\"F_{1/2}(x)\")\nplot!(x,FermiDiracIntegralNorm.(3/2,x),label=L\"F_{3/2}(x)\")\nplot!(x,FermiDiracIntegralNorm.(5/2,x),label=L\"F_{5/2}(x)\")\nxlabel!(L\"x\")\ntitle!(\"Fermi-Dirac Integral\")"},{"title":"Bose–Einstein integrals","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Bose–Einstein-integrals","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"BoseEinsteinIntegral evaluates the unnormalized complete integral"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"    mathcalB_k(eta) = int_0^infty fract^kexp(t-eta)-1dt"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"for integer or half-integer orders k > -1 and real η ≤ 0. BoseEinsteinIntegralNorm evaluates its normalized form"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"    B_k(eta) = fracmathcalB_k(eta)Gamma(k+1)\n              = operatornameLi_k+1(e^eta)"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The normalized function also accepts integer or half-integer orders down to k = -9/2. For k ≤ -1, it is defined by repeated differentiation, dB_kdeta = B_k-1; the integral above does not converge at those orders."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"Both functions return 0 at η = -Inf. At η = 0, the normalized value is zeta(k+1) for k > 0, and the unnormalized value includes the factor Gamma(k+1). For supported k ≤ 0, the limit as η → 0⁻ is Inf. Positive η, NaN, and unsupported orders raise DomainError."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"julia> using FewSpecialFunctions\n\njulia> BoseEinsteinIntegralNorm(0.5, -1.0) ≈ 0.4284407345998379\ntrue\n\njulia> BoseEinsteinIntegral(1.5, -1.0) ≈ (3 * sqrt(π) / 4) * 0.3957280103803376\ntrue\n\njulia> BoseEinsteinIntegralNorm(1, 0) ≈ π^2 / 6\ntrue\n\njulia> BoseEinsteinIntegralNorm(-1, -1.0) ≈ inv(expm1(1.0))\ntrue\n\njulia> all(isfinite, BoseEinsteinIntegralNorm.(0.5, [-2.0, -1.0, 0.0]))\ntrue"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"For Float32 and Float64, the implementation uses minimax rational approximations with convergent-series fallbacks, based on Tables A.5–A.48 of Fukushima's 2020 preprint for half-integer orders -9/2:1:39/2 and integer orders 1:19. The approximations target near-double-precision accuracy for Float64; Float32 results retain single precision. Nonpositive integer orders use elementary formulas, and higher orders use convergent series. BigFloat evaluation uses convergent series at the working precision."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"ForwardDiff differentiates with respect to η using the order-lowering identity. Differentiation with respect to the discrete order k raises DomainError."},{"title":"Clausen functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Clausen-functions","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The Clausen functions are implemented for orders 1 through 6, using a combination of series summation and analytic continuation. The code is based on the methods described in this paper."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"Clausen(n, θ): Computes the Clausen function of order n at angle θ.\nF_clausen, f_n, Ci_complex: Auxiliary functions for advanced use and analytic continuation."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings # hide\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" # hide\n\nplot_font = \"Computer Modern\" # hide\ndefault(fontfamily=plot_font,linewidth=2.5, framestyle=:box, label=nothing, grid=true,palette=:tab10) # hide\n\nθ = collect(range(0, 2π, length=100))\n\nplot(θ,Clausen.(1, θ), label=L\"C_1(θ)\")\nplot!(θ,Clausen.(2, θ), label=L\"C_2(θ)\")\nplot!(θ,Clausen.(3, θ), label=L\"C_3(θ)\")\nplot!(θ,Clausen.(4, θ), label=L\"C_4(θ)\")\n\nxlabel!(L\"\\theta\")\ntitle!(\"Clausen Functions\")"},{"title":"Fresnel integrals","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Fresnel-integrals","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The Fresnel integrals are"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"C(z) = int_0^z cosleft(fracpi t^22right)mathrmdt\nqquad\nS(z) = int_0^z sinleft(fracpi t^22right)mathrmdt"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"fresnel(z) returns (C(z), S(z), C(z) + im * S(z)). The convenience functions FresnelC, FresnelS, and FresnelE return the corresponding components. Real and complex arguments are supported; integer arguments are promoted to floating point."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using FewSpecialFunctions\n\nC, S, E = fresnel(1.0)\nFresnelC(1 + im)\nFresnelS(1 + im)\nFresnelE(1 + im)"},{"title":"Real axis","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Real-axis","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings # hide\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" # hide\n\ndefault(fontfamily=\"Computer Modern\", linewidth=2.5, framestyle=:box, grid=true)\nx = range(-6, 6, length=1000)\nplot(x, FresnelC.(x), label=L\"C(x)\", xlabel=L\"x\", ylabel=\"value\",\n     title=\"Fresnel integrals on the real axis\")\nplot!(x, FresnelS.(x), label=L\"S(x)\")"},{"title":"Euler spiral","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Euler-spiral","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings # hide\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" # hide\n\ndefault(fontfamily=\"Computer Modern\", linewidth=2.5, framestyle=:box, grid=true)\nx = range(-12, 12, length=1500)\nplot(FresnelC.(x), FresnelS.(x), label=nothing, xlabel=L\"C(x)\",\n     ylabel=L\"S(x)\", title=\"Euler spiral\")"},{"title":"Complex plane","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Complex-plane","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using DomainColoring, FewSpecialFunctions\n\ndomaincolor(FresnelE, [-2, 2, -2, 2], grid=true)"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The implementation combines a convergent series near zero, stable intermediate evaluation, and asymptotic expansions for large arguments. It is adapted from this paper and the upstream Fortran source repository."},{"title":"Dawson integral","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/#Dawson-integral","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"D(x) = e^-x^2int_0^x e^t^2mathrmdt"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"dawson(x) evaluates the real Dawson integral. It is odd, is zero at the origin, and approaches 1 / (2x) for large magnitude arguments."},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings # hide\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\" # hide\n\ndefault(fontfamily=\"Computer Modern\", linewidth=2.5, framestyle=:box, grid=true)\nx = range(-8, 8, length=1000)\nplot(x, FewSpecialFunctions.dawson.(x), label=L\"D(x)\", xlabel=L\"x\", ylabel=\"value\", title=\"Dawson integral\")"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Functions","location":"Functions/","category":"page","text":"The implementation uses the adaptive series and continued fractions described in Zaghloul (2023) and is informed by the upstream MIT-licensed Fortran implementation."},{"title":"References","page":"References","location":"API/#References","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"References","page":"References","location":"API/","category":"page","text":"Clausen functions: Quadrature processes for efficient calculation of the Clausen functions\nCoulomb wave functions: Connection formulas between Coulomb wave functions\nDebye functions: Calculation of Integer and Noninteger n-Dimensional Debye Functions Using Binomial Coefficients and Incomplete Gamma Functions\nFermi-Dirac integrals: Notes on Fermi-Dirac Integrals\nBose–Einstein integrals: Toshio Fukushima, Analytical computation of Bose-Einstein integral of half integer orders, −9/2, −7/2, …, and 39/2, and integer orders, 1, 2, …, and 19, by minimax rational function approximations (2020 preprint)\nFresnel integrals: Calculation of Fresnel integrals of real and complex arguments up to 28 significant digits\nDawson integral: Numerical calculation of Dawson's integral and its imaginary error functions of complex arguments for arbitrary value of the phase angle\nMarcum Q-function: https://arxiv.org/pdf/1311.0681v1\nParabolic cylinder functions"},{"title":"API","page":"References","location":"API/#API","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.η","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.η","category":"function","text":"η(a::Number, k::Number)\nη(ϵ::Number)\n\nCoulomb parameter. For two arguments, returns 1/(a*k). For one argument, returns 1/sqrt(ϵ).\n\nBoth a and k must be nonzero. ϵ must be nonzero.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.C","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.C","category":"function","text":"C(ℓ::Number, η::Number)\n\nCoulomb normalization constant.\n\nFor complex parameters, the square root of the gamma product is defined by exp((loggamma(ℓ + 1 + im * η) + loggamma(ℓ + 1 - im * η)) / 2). This selects a local analytic branch using the principal log-gamma branches, away from their cuts and poles, and agrees with the positive normalization for real ℓ ≥ 0 and real η. The full normalization is evaluated in logarithmic form to avoid overflow or underflow of its individual factors.\n\nSee Eq. (8) of the implementation paper and DLMF 33.2.5.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.θ","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.θ","category":"function","text":"θ(ℓ::Number, η::Number, ρ::Number)\n\nCoulomb phase function.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.F","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.F","category":"function","text":"F(ℓ::Number, η::Number, ρ::Number)\n\nRegular Coulomb wave function.\n\nReferences:\n\nCoulomb wave function\nImplementation paper\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.D⁺","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.D⁺","category":"function","text":"D⁺(ℓ::Number, η::Number)\n\nCoulomb D⁺ normalization factor.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.D⁻","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.D⁻","category":"function","text":"D⁻(ℓ::Number, η::Number)\n\nCoulomb D⁻ normalization factor.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.H⁺","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.H⁺","category":"function","text":"H⁺(ℓ::Number, η::Number, ρ::Number)\n\nOutgoing Coulomb wave function.\n\nReferences:\n\nCoulomb wave function\nImplementation paper\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.H⁻","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.H⁻","category":"function","text":"H⁻(ℓ::Number, η::Number, ρ::Number)\n\nIncoming Coulomb wave function.\n\nReferences:\n\nCoulomb wave function\nImplementation paper\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.F_imag","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.F_imag","category":"function","text":"F_imag(ℓ::Number, η::Number, ρ::Number)\n\nImaginary part of the regular Coulomb wave function.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.G","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.G","category":"function","text":"G(ℓ::Number, η::Number, ρ::Number)\n\nIrregular Coulomb wave function.\n\nReferences:\n\nCoulomb wave function\nImplementation paper\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.M_regularized","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.M_regularized","category":"function","text":"M_regularized(α::Number, β::Number, γ::Number)\n\nRegularized confluent hypergeometric function.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.Φ","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.Φ","category":"function","text":"Φ(ℓ::Number, η::Number, ρ::Number)\n\nModified Coulomb function Φ.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.w","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.w","category":"function","text":"w(ℓ::Integer, η::Number)\nw(ℓ::Number, η::Number)\n\nAuxiliary function for Coulomb wave functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.w_plus","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.w_plus","category":"function","text":"w_plus(ℓ::Number, η::Number)\n\nAuxiliary function for Coulomb wave functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.w_minus","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.w_minus","category":"function","text":"w_minus(ℓ::Number, η::Number)\n\nAuxiliary function for Coulomb wave functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.h_plus","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.h_plus","category":"function","text":"h_plus(ℓ::Number, η::Number)\n\nAuxiliary function for Coulomb wave functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.h_minus","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.h_minus","category":"function","text":"h_minus(ℓ::Number, η::Number)\n\nAuxiliary function for Coulomb wave functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.g","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.g","category":"function","text":"g(ℓ::Number, η::Number)\n\nAuxiliary function for Coulomb wave functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.Φ_dot","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.Φ_dot","category":"function","text":"Φ_dot(ℓ::Number, η::Number, ρ::Number; h=nothing)\n\nNumerical derivative of Φ with respect to ℓ using a central finite difference. h is the step size; defaults to cbrt(eps(T)) where T is the float type of ℓ, which is near-optimal for double-sided finite differences.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.F_dot","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.F_dot","category":"function","text":"F_dot(ℓ::Number, η::Number, ρ::Number; h=nothing)\n\nNumerical derivative of F with respect to ℓ using a central finite difference. h is the step size; defaults to cbrt(eps(T)) where T is the float type of ℓ.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.Ψ","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.Ψ","category":"function","text":"Ψ(ℓ::Number, η::Number, ρ::Number; h=nothing)\n\nAuxiliary function for Coulomb wave functions. h is the finite-difference step size; defaults to cbrt(eps(T)) (see Φ_dot).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.I","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.I","category":"function","text":"I(ℓ::Number, η::Number, ρ::Number; h=nothing)\n\nAuxiliary function for Coulomb wave functions. h is the finite-difference step size; defaults to cbrt(eps(T)) (see Φ_dot).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.debye_function","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.debye_function","category":"function","text":"debye_function(n::T, β::T, x::T; tol=1e-35, max_terms=2000) where {T <: AbstractFloat}\n\nCompute n/x^n * ∫₀ˣ t^n/(exp(t)-1)^β dt for finite n > 0, 0 < β < n+1, and x ≥ 0. At x = 0, returns the limiting value: 0 for β < 1, 1 for β = 1, and Inf for β > 1. Returns 0 at x = Inf. Supports any AbstractFloat type (e.g., Float32, Float64, BigFloat). Array broadcasting is supported: any one argument may be an AbstractArray.\n\nThrows ArgumentError for invalid parameters, tolerances, or work limits. Throws ErrorException if quadrature cannot meet the requested tolerance within the work limit.\n\ntol: positive finite relative quadrature tolerance (default 1e-35), floored at 8eps(T)\nmax_terms: positive integer limit on quadrature subintervals (default 2000)\n\nReferences:\n\nDebye function\nPaper\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.fresnel","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.fresnel","category":"function","text":"fresnel(z::Number)\n\nCompute the Fresnel cosine and sine integrals and their combination:\n\nS(z) = ∫₀ᶻ sin(π / 2 * t²) dt\nC(z) = ∫₀ᶻ cos(π / 2 * t²) dt\n\nReturns (C, S, C + im * S). For complex inputs, C and S are the analytic complex Fresnel integrals. For ComplexF16, ComplexF32, and ComplexF64, the third value is evaluated independently to avoid cancellation. Other complex float types retain the series/asymptotic implementation, whose combination can lose precision when C and S nearly cancel.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.FresnelC","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.FresnelC","category":"function","text":"FresnelC(z::Number)\n\nCompute the Fresnel cosine integral C(z).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.FresnelS","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.FresnelS","category":"function","text":"FresnelS(z::Number)\n\nCompute the Fresnel sine integral S(z).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.FresnelE","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.FresnelE","category":"function","text":"FresnelE(z::Number)\n\nCompute the Fresnel integral combination C(z) + im * S(z).\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.Clausen","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.Clausen","category":"function","text":"Clausen(n::Int, θ::Real; N::Int=10, m::Int=20)\n\nCompute the Clausen function Cl_n(θ) of order n at angle θ. Supports any Real input type for θ.\n\nn must be in 1..6; N must be 10 or 20 (throws ArgumentError otherwise).\n\nNote on singularity: Clausen(1, θ) = -log|2sin(θ/2)| has a logarithmic singularity at θ = 0, 2π, 4π, …, where it returns Inf.\n\nN: number of Gauss-Legendre quadrature nodes used in the Euler-Maclaurin tail sum; 10 gives double precision, 20 gives extended precision.\nm: number of terms in the direct summation before switching to the tail formula.\n\nReferences:\n\nClausen function\nImplementation paper\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.Ci_complex","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.Ci_complex","category":"function","text":"Ci_complex(z::Complex{T}) where {T <: AbstractFloat}\n\nComplex cosine integral function used in Clausen function calculations. Supports any AbstractFloat type.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.f_n","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.f_n","category":"function","text":"f_n(n::Int, k::Int, θ::Real)\n\nCompute the Clausen series summand fₙ(k, θ):     sin(kθ)/kⁿ for even n,     cos(kθ)/kⁿ for odd n. Supports any Real input type for θ.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.F_clausen","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.F_clausen","category":"function","text":"F_clausen(n::Int, z::Complex{T}, θ::T) where {T <: AbstractFloat}\n\nDispatch to the correct primitive function Fₙ(z, θ) for n = 1..6. Supports any AbstractFloat type.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.FermiDiracIntegral","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.FermiDiracIntegral","category":"function","text":"FermiDiracIntegral(j, x)\n\nThe Fermi-Dirac integral\n\nReturns the value F_j(x)\n\nSupports any AbstractFloat type (e.g., Float32, Float64, BigFloat).\n\nResources: [1] D. Bednarczyk and J. Bednarczyk, Phys. Lett. A, 64, 409 (1978) [2] J. S. Blakemore, Solid-St. Electron, 25, 1067 (1982) [3] X. Aymerich-Humet, F. Serra-Mestres, and J. Millan, Solid-St. Electron, 24, 981 (1981) [4] X. Aymerich-Humet, F. Serra-Mestres, and J. Millan, J. Appl. Phys., 54, 2850 (1983) [5] H. M. Antia, Rational Function Approximations for Fermi-Dirac Integrals (1993)\n\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/0811.0116 https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CompleteFermi%E2%80%93Diracintegral https://dlmf.nist.gov/25.12#iii\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.FermiDiracIntegralNorm","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.FermiDiracIntegralNorm","category":"function","text":"FermiDiracIntegralNorm(j,x)\n\nThe Fermi-Dirac integral\n\n    F_j(x) = frac1Gamma(j+1)int_0^infty fract^jexp(t-x)+1  dt\n\nReturns the value F_j(x)\n\nResources: [1] D. Bednarczyk and J. Bednarczyk, Phys. Lett. A, 64, 409 (1978) [2] J. S. Blakemore, Solid-St. Electron, 25, 1067 (1982) [3] X. Aymerich-Humet, F. Serra-Mestres, and J. Millan, Solid-St. Electron, 24, 981 (1981) [4] X. Aymerich-Humet, F. Serra-Mestres, and J. Millan, J. Appl. Phys., 54, 2850 (1983) [5] H. M. Antia, Rational Function Approximations for Fermi-Dirac Integrals (1993)\n\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/0811.0116 https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermi-Dirac-Integral https://dlmf.nist.gov/25.12#iii\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.BoseEinsteinIntegral","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.BoseEinsteinIntegral","category":"function","text":"BoseEinsteinIntegral(k, η)\n\nCompute the unnormalized Bose–Einstein integral\n\nint_0^infty fract^kexp(t-eta)-1dt\n= Gamma(k+1) B_k(eta)\n\nAccepts integer and half-integer orders k > -1 and real η ≤ 0. At η = 0, returns gamma(k+1)*zeta(k+1) for k > 0, and Inf for k = -1/2 or k = 0. Returns zero at η = -Inf. Invalid inputs throw DomainError. Supports Float32, Float64, BigFloat, dot broadcasting, and ForwardDiff differentiation with respect to η.\n\nThe naming follows FermiDiracIntegral. For normalized values and continuation to negative orders, use BoseEinsteinIntegralNorm.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.BoseEinsteinIntegralNorm","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.BoseEinsteinIntegralNorm","category":"function","text":"BoseEinsteinIntegralNorm(k, η)\n\nCompute the normalized Bose–Einstein integral\n\nB_k(eta) = frac1Gamma(k+1)int_0^infty\nfract^kexp(t-eta)-1dt = operatornameLi_k+1(e^eta)\n\nAccepts integer and half-integer orders k ≥ -9/2 and real η ≤ 0. For k ≤ -1, the polylogarithm defines the continuation by differentiation; the displayed integral itself requires k > -1. At η = 0, returns zeta(k+1) for k > 0 and Inf otherwise. Returns zero at η = -Inf. Invalid orders, positive η, and NaN inputs throw DomainError.\n\nUses Fukushima's minimax rational approximations for half-integer orders -9/2:1:39/2 and integer orders 1:19, elementary formulas for nonpositive integers, and a convergent series for higher orders. Float32 and Float64 use the double precision coefficients; BigFloat uses convergent series at the working precision. The ForwardDiff extension supports differentiation with respect to η, but not the discrete order k.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> round(BoseEinsteinIntegralNorm(0.5, -1.0); digits=12)\n0.4284407346\n\nReference\n\nT. Fukushima (2020 preprint), Analytical computation of Bose–Einstein integral of half integer orders, −9/2, −7/2, ⋯, and 39/2, and integer orders, 1, 2, ⋯, and 19, by minimax rational function approximations, Eqs. (11)–(24), Tables A.3–A.48, doi:10.13140/RG.2.2.21720.65283.\n\nSee also BoseEinsteinIntegral, FermiDiracIntegralNorm.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.MarcumQ","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.MarcumQ","category":"function","text":"MarcumQ(μ::Real, a::Real, b::Real)\n\nCompute the generalized Marcum Q-function Q_μ(a, b) of order μ with non-centrality parameter a ≥ 0 and threshold b ≥ 0. Returns a value in [0, 1].\n\nRequires μ ≥ 0.5, a ≥ 0, and b ≥ 0; throws ArgumentError otherwise. Supports any Real input type; arguments are promoted to a common floating-point type. Array broadcasting is supported: any one argument may be an AbstractArray.\n\nReference:     [1] https://arxiv.org/pdf/1311.0681v1\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.dQdb","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.dQdb","category":"function","text":"dQdb(M, a, b)\n\nDerivative ∂Q_M(a,b)/∂b of the (standard) Marcum Q-function of order M. Requires M integer ≥1 and a>0.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.voigt","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.voigt","category":"function","text":"voigt(x::Real, y::Real)\n\nEvaluate the real Voigt function\n\nK(x y) = fracypi int_-infty^infty\n    frace^-t^2(x - t)^2 + y^2  mathrmdt\nqquad y ge 0\n\nFor y == 0, K(x, 0) = exp(-x^2). The implementation follows the Fourier-expansion method of Abrarov, Quine, and Jagpal.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.U","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.U","category":"function","text":"U(a::T, x::T) where {T <: AbstractFloat}\n\nCompute the real parabolic cylinder function U(a,x). Supports Float32, Float64, and BigFloat. Uses a precision-guarded convergent series when the order and argument do not permit an accurate asymptotic expansion.\n\nReference: DLMF, Chapter 12.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.V","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.V","category":"function","text":"V(a::T, x::T) where {T <: AbstractFloat}\n\nCompute the real parabolic cylinder function V(a,x) using a convergent series with extra working precision. Supports Float32, Float64, and BigFloat.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.W","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.W","category":"function","text":"W(a::T, x::T) where {T <: AbstractFloat}\n\nCompute the real parabolic cylinder function W(a,x). Supports Float32, Float64, and BigFloat. Uses a precision-guarded convergent series or an asymptotic expansion whose decreasing terms reach the requested precision.\n\nReference: DLMF 12.14.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.dU","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.dU","category":"function","text":"dU(a::T, x::T) where {T <: AbstractFloat}\n\nCompute ∂U(a,x)/∂x from the same expansion as U. Supports Float32, Float64, and BigFloat.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.dV","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.dV","category":"function","text":"dV(a::T, x::T) where {T <: AbstractFloat}\n\nCompute ∂V(a,x)/∂x from the same convergent series as V. Supports Float32, Float64, and BigFloat.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.dW","page":"References","location":"API/#FewSpecialFunctions.dW","category":"function","text":"dW(a::T, x::T) where {T <: AbstractFloat}\n\nCompute ∂W(a,x)/∂x from the same expansion as W. Supports Float32, Float64, and BigFloat.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"title":"Dawson integral","page":"References","location":"API/#Dawson-integral","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"References","page":"References","location":"API/","category":"page","text":"dawson(x::Real) evaluates the real Dawson integral and preserves Float32, Float64, and BigFloat inputs. Integer and other real inputs are promoted to floating point. The implementation is related to the imaginary error function erfi(x), but this documentation expresses that relation in prose rather than through a cross-reference."},{"title":"FewSpecialFunctions.jl","page":"Home","location":"#FewSpecialFunctions.jl","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Home","page":"Home","location":"","category":"page","text":"Some special functions."},{"title":"Installation","page":"Home","location":"#Installation","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Home","page":"Home","location":"","category":"page","text":"Get the latest stable release with Julia's package manager:"},{"title":"Home","page":"Home","location":"","category":"page","text":"julia ] add FewSpecialFunctions"},{"title":"Features","page":"Home","location":"#Features","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Home","page":"Home","location":"","category":"page","text":"Numerical implementations of special mathematical functions\nGeneric type support: all functions work with Float32, Float64, BigFloat, and other AbstractFloat types\nAutomatic type promotion for mixed inputs"},{"title":"Quick example","page":"Home","location":"#Quick-example","category":"section","text":""},{"title":"Home","page":"Home","location":"","category":"page","text":"Here is how to generate an Euler spiral using FewSpecialFunctions.jl. "},{"title":"Home","page":"Home","location":"","category":"page","text":"using Plots, FewSpecialFunctions, LaTeXStrings \n\nplot_font = \"Computer Modern\"\ndefault(\n    fontfamily=plot_font,\n    linewidth=2.5, \n    framestyle=:box, \n    label=nothing, \n    grid=true,\n    palette=:tab10,\n)\n\nx = range(-25,25,length=5000)\n\nxlabel!(L\"C(x)\")\nylabel!(L\"S(x)\")\ntitle!(\"Euler Spiral\")\nplot(FresnelC.(x),FresnelS.(x))"}]
}
